<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted. This file exists to say so deliberately, and to
  record why, because the alternative is a product that silently inverts itself.

  WHAT BREAKS WITHOUT IT. `targetSdk` is 36 and Android has denied cleartext by
  default since API 28. Every local model endpoint jojo offers is plain HTTP and
  always will be: Ollama serves `http://localhost:11434`, vLLM and LM Studio
  serve `http://<the machine on your desk>:8000/v1`, and the document reader
  serves `http://127.0.0.1:3001/mcp`. None ships a certificate. With cleartext
  denied, the release APK can reach the four CLOUD providers and none of the
  local ones — so the private option is the one that breaks, and it breaks ONLY
  in release, because `src/debug/AndroidManifest.xml` sets
  `usesCleartextTraffic="true"` and every developer therefore sees it working.

  WHY NOT AN ALLOWLIST OF PRIVATE RANGES, which is the obvious answer and does
  not work. `<domain>` matches a hostname suffix or an IP LITERAL, exactly.
  There is no CIDR form, and `includeSubdomains` applies to DNS labels rather
  than to address prefixes — so `192.168.0.0` matches the host called
  "192.168.0.0" and nothing else. An allowlist here could cover `localhost` and
  `127.0.0.1` and would still refuse the case the feature is for: a phone
  talking to a desktop across the room at whatever address the router handed it.

  WHAT CARRIES THE SECURITY INSTEAD. jojo never picks an `http://` endpoint on
  a person's behalf. The scheme comes from a URL they typed into Settings, and
  the transfer dialler separately refuses anything that is not a private address
  (`service/kg/core/dial.ts`). This permits cleartext where the user asked for
  cleartext; it does not permit a silent downgrade of anything jojo chose.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
